avoid toxicity or reduced efficacy.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Multiple protease inhibitors are associated with urinary stone formation\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Urinary (or kidney) stones represent a documented adverse effect of several protease inhibitors used in HIV combination therapy. Specifically, ritonavir, indinavir, nelfinavir, amprenavir, and lopinavir have been linked to this complication [Data: Relationships (16086)]. To mitigate this risk, clinicians recommend maintaining high fluid intake—over 2000 mL per day—to ensure adequate hydration and reduce stone formation likelihood [Data: Relationships (16086)]. This side effect underscores the importance of patient education and proactive monitoring during PI-based regimens.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Protease inhibitors significantly alter plasma concentrations of co-administered drugs\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Protease inhibitors profoundly affect the pharmacokinetics of other medications by inhibiting or inducing cytochrome P450 enzymes, particularly CYP3A4. For instance, PIs increase calcineurin inhibitor (CNI) blood levels, potentially leading to acute kidney injury, and elevate erythromycin plasma concentrations, raising the risk of cardiac arrhythmias and sudden death [Data: Relationships (4272, 4274)]. Similarly, amprenavir reduces methadone blood concentration to approximately 35% of baseline, triggering opioid withdrawal symptoms in some patients [Data: Relationships (1862)]. These interactions mandate dose adjustments, alternative agent selection, or enhanced therapeutic monitoring when PIs are part of a treatment regimen.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Nelfinavir is contraindicated in late-pregnancy patients\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Nelfinavir, a protease inhibitor metabolized primarily by CYP3A4, is explicitly contraindicated for use in patients in the third trimester of pregnancy [Data: Entities (4976); Relationships (6186)]. This restriction likely stems from altered pharmacokinetics during pregnancy or potential fetal risks, though the provided evidence focuses on its metabolic profile and strong CYP3A4 inhibition, which also increases the risk of rhabdomyolysis when co-administered with simvastatin [Data: Relationships (6186)]. This contraindication highlights the need for tailored antiretroviral strategies in pregnant individuals.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Interactions between protease inhibitors and dietary supplements or non-prescription agents pose hidden risks\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The concurrent use of food supplements and over-the-counter products with protease inhibitor–based antiretroviral therapy introduces significant, often underrecognized, drug interaction risks. A Danish study found that 52% of HIV patients on PI-containing regimens had medication lists indicating potential DDIs requiring dose adjustment or monitoring [Data: Relationships (50479)]. Researchers emphasized the importance of routinely inquiring about supplement use in people living with HIV, as these agents can interfere with antiretroviral efficacy or safety through mechanisms involving CYP450 enzymes or drug transporters [Data: Relationships (50479)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Substance use can compromise protease inhibitor efficacy and lead to treatment failure\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The misuse of psychoactive substances—including alcohol, cannabis, methamphetamine, heroin, benzodiazepines, and opioids—can negatively interact with protease inhibitors, potentially diminishing their antiviral effect and resulting in treatment failure [Data: Entities (11499); Relationships (15054)]. These interactions may occur through pharmacokinetic interference or behavioral factors affecting adherence. Additionally, even common supplements like vitamin C have been reported to reduce indinavir plasma concentrations sufficiently to cause virologic failure [Data: Relationships (15054)]. This finding stresses the need for comprehensive patient histories and integrated addiction support in HIV care.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Darunavir/cobicistat belongs to a high-risk class of pharmacokinetically boosted antiretrovirals\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Darunavir/cobicistat (DRV/c) is classified as a pharmacokinetically (PK) enhanced antiretroviral drug, where cobicistat acts as a potent CYP3A4 and drug transporter inhibitor to boost darunavir exposure [Data: Entities (8694); Relationships (59170)]. While effective, such boosted regimens are major contributors to clinically significant DDIs. One large study of 25,919 treatment-naïve patients found that regimens containing PK enhancers were most frequently associated with contraindicated co-prescriptions, including dangerous combinations with budesonide, phenobarbital, or rifampin [Data: Relationships (59170)]. This reinforces the necessity of consulting authoritative DDI databases (e.g., University of Liverpool’s HIV Drug Interactions resource) before prescribing.\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 7.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The community poses a high clinical impact due to the life-threatening drug interactions, contraindications, and treatment failure risks associated with protease inhibitor use in vulnerable populations.\"\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1782121313, "model": "qwen3-max", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1551, "prompt_tokens": 6949, "total_tokens": 8500, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 1408}}, "formatted_response": null, "cont